#### In the output observed, we found that ArrayList.Contains method does not adhere to the contract for its return value. We also see that the precondition (list count should be equal or greater than 0) fails for some test runs which means the Add method did not increment the count of the list.

##### Proposed solutions to fix the problem:
1. Modify the code in the "PUT_ContainsContract" method to add specific pre- and post-conditions related to the return value of the ArrayList.Contains method.
2. Use another testing tool, perhaps even from Microsoft's Pex, to execute the test cases, that provides fine-grained control and customizable test cases creation for complex code.
3. Check the ArrayList implementation whether it doesn't have any logical errors. The problem may lie in there. 

For the purpose of this course, we'll continue to use Pex to ensure our contracts are robust and we can easily generate unit test cases for our methods. This is just a sample task and actual implementation may differ based on ArrayList implementation.

**Note: It is noteworthy to mention that automatic code review and static analysis tools can help us detect contract and code issues. However, tools cannot identify all possible scenarios and bugs, therefore it's important to build your own test-cases.**

## Conclusion
Using automated testing (like Pex) can help us to find contract and code issues, but it is still manual to check and validate complex code. Moreover, testing is an integral part of development and should be carried out at every stage of development for all the components of the software system.

Even when we use automated testing, we should always design our solutions so that they are robust, reliable, and easy to understand and maintain. Automated testing tools can help us write more reliable and efficient tests, but it is up to the developer to write good test cases to validate the behavior of their code. They should consider the worst-case scenarios when designing their test cases and try to validate the edge cases.

Overall, good unit testing practice enhances comprehension of program behavior, makes fixing bugs easier, and decreases the likelihood of integration issues. It is best to have a well-structured test plan and perform regular testing at each level of the development process.

## Additional resources
1. Books: "Test-Driven Development: By Example" book by Kent Beck, "Agile Software Testing: Foundations, Principles, and Practices" by Krzysztof Jurga, etc.
2. Online courses: Coursera, Udemy, etc.
3. Testing frameworks: NUnit, xUnit, Moq, etc.
4. Tutorial sites: Tutorials point, W3schools, etc.
5. Bug tracking: JIRA, BugHerd, etc.
6. Blogs: Test Automation Geek, QA.com, etc.
7. Communities: StackOverflow, Reddit, etc.

## Further learning 
Once you've got a grasp of how unit tests work and how to write them, there's a lot more you can do. You can learn to do more complex things like use testing frameworks to organize your tests, write integration tests, perform load and stress testing, and do everything else that a quality software tester does.

To become a fully fledged unit tester, you'd need to understand both the functional requirements of an application and the internal workings of that application itself. Learning about software architecture, design patterns, and best practices will also be beneficial. Good understanding of language and platform-specifics is a necessity for effective testing. Lastly but certainly not least, patience and passion for testing is the key.

## Happy testing ✅
